>                      calculation, which means slooooow for a
>             maximum
>                      likelihood search.
>
>                      With a really zippy computer you might
>             do a little
>                      better, but I think matrix
>             exponentiation is
>                      quadratic or worse with # of states.
>
>                      It would be even slower with 3309 taxa,
>             but speed
>                      should be linear with # of taxa, the
>             problem is the
>                      matrix size.
>
>
>
>                      Options:
>
>                      1. Reduce the size of the state space
>             by limiting
>                      the maximum # of areas.  Use:
>
>                      numstates_from_numareas(12, 12)
>                      numstates_from_numareas(12, 3)
>
>                      ...to see how this works.
>
>                      This is defensible especially if no
>             species lives in
>                      e.g. more than 3 areas.  One could also
>             manually
>                      list all desired states, all this would
>             take is some
>                      R skillz.
>
>
>
>                      2. Try the sparse matrix exponetiation
>             routines with
>                      use_sparse=TRUE.  Theoretically, sparse
>             matrix
>                      routines can handle much bigger
>             matrices.  I
>                      implemented this in rexpokit, but
>             haven't had much
>                      luck, the calculations that result
>             appear to be more
>                      approximate, so in an ML search I get
>             different
>                      answers. In a Bayesian analysis maybe
>             it would be
>                      OK.  You/we would probably have to
>             tweak the
>                      BioGeoBEARS functions manually since I
>             didn't
>                      implement all the corresponding
>             calculations for
>                      sparse matrices in BioGeoBEARS.
>
>
>                      3. Use the BayArea C++ program,
>             realizing that it
>                      has no cladogenesis model and my model
>             testing
>                      suggests that this is almost always
>             very poorly
>                      performing compared to e.g. DEC+J in
>             terms of AIC etc.
>
>                      Cheers!
