>>                       The problem here is the number of areas.
>>
>>                       12 areas equals...
>>
>>                       = 2^12 possible combinations of
>>              presence/absence in
>>                       each area
>>
>>                       = 2^12 states = 4096 "states" (think of
>>              a character
>>                       with 4096 states, that's what
>>              BioGeoBEARS/LAGRANGE
>>                       are doing)
>>
>>                       = a transition matrix of 4096x4096
>>
>>                       = 16,777,216 cells in the transition
>>              matrix that
>>                       have to be stored just to represent the
>>              matrix in
>>                       memory.
>>
>>                       Then the matrix has to be exponentiated
>>              to calculate
>>                       likelihoods on the branches.
>>
>>                       In my experience, if transition
>>              matrices get bigger
>>                       than about 550x550, the matrix
>>              exponentiation takes
>>                        >1 second per exponentiation, which
>>              means a tree
>>                       with 100 branches takes >100 seconds
>>              per likelihood
>>                       calculation, which means slooooow for a
>>              maximum
>>                       likelihood search.
>>
>>                       With a really zippy computer you might
>>              do a little
>>                       better, but I think matrix
>>              exponentiation is
>>                       quadratic or worse with # of states.
>>
>>                       It would be even slower with 3309 taxa,
>>              but speed
>>                       should be linear with # of taxa, the
>>              problem is the
>>                       matrix size.
>>
>>
